WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.708
A secure socket roadmap begins with the trust boundary.

2
00:00:03.708 --> 00:00:09.063
Treat network input as untrusted, even when it arrives from an expected component.

3
00:00:09.063 --> 00:00:16.066
Define the protocol contract clearly: message framing, allowed commands, encoding, size limits, version behavior, authentication expectations, and

4
00:00:16.066 --> 00:00:17.302
safe failure behavior.

5
00:00:17.302 --> 00:00:23.893
TCP byte streams and datagram protocols each need explicit handling that matches their real delivery semantics.

6
00:00:23.893 --> 00:00:26.365
Exposure and lifecycle decisions come next.

7
00:00:26.365 --> 00:00:34.604
Bind only where the service needs to be reachable, document ports and deployment assumptions, and make service discovery behavior intentional.

8
00:00:34.604 --> 00:00:40.783
Represent socket ownership with RAII so cleanup is reliable across normal completion and error paths.

9
00:00:40.783 --> 00:00:48.198
Accept loops, cancellation, shutdown, and cross-platform handle rules should be part of the design rather than afterthoughts.

10
00:00:48.198 --> 00:00:53.142
Reliable I/O and defensive parsing are the middle of the roadmap.

11
00:00:53.142 --> 00:00:57.261
Handle partial reads, partial writes, blocking behavior, disconnects, and timeouts.

12
00:00:57.261 --> 00:01:03.028
Validate lengths, counts, encodings, identifiers, and message structure before allocating or making business decisions.

13
00:01:03.028 --> 00:01:10.855
Apply resource limits, bounded queues, connection limits, request limits, and backpressure so the service can fail gracefully under pressure.

14
00:01:10.855 --> 00:01:14.975
Finally, protect the channel, observe safely, and verify the design.

15
00:01:14.975 --> 00:01:21.978
Use approved TLS libraries with certificate and hostname validation when sensitive data or untrusted networks are involved.

16
00:01:21.978 --> 00:01:25.686
Log useful events without exposing secrets or private payloads.

17
00:01:25.686 --> 00:01:30.629
Test malformed input, disconnects, partial I/O, timeouts, TLS failures, and shutdown.

18
00:01:30.629 --> 00:01:39.280
Review socket code as security-sensitive because network-facing behavior often becomes part of the application's reliability and security surface.
